,["e/e8/Rapsodia_Lezhe_cookies.jpg","Rapsodia Lezhe cookies","Arianit","CC BY-SA 4.0",0.75],["e/e3/Dion_Hasani.jpg","Dion Hasani","Albert xhukaj","CC BY-SA 4.0",1.45]],"a":["Shëngjin","Shëngjin (Albanian pronunciation: [ʃəɲˈɟin]) is a coastal resort town and a former municipality in Lezhë County, northwestern Albania. At the 2015 local government reform, it became a subdivision of the municipality of Lezhë. The population at the 2023 census was 6,963. Shëngjin is a growing tourist destination, well known for its beaches and resort accommodations. Shëngjin is one of many cities within the District of Lezhë and is home to one of Albania's entry ports, Port of Shëngjin."]},"2782":{"n":"Marina e Orikumit","p":[["3/32/Karaburun%2C_cesta_II.jpg","Karaburun, cesta II","I would appreciate being notified if yo…","CC BY 4.0",0.67],["f/f6/Karaburun%2C_pohled_na_Orikumsk%C3%BD_z%C3%A1liv.jpg","Karaburun, pohled na Orikumský záliv","I would appreciate being notified if yo…","CC BY 4.0",0.67],["7/70/Vlor%C3%AB_Al_-_panoramio_%282%29.jpg","Vlorë Al","Thanas Todhe (Guri Q…","CC BY 3.0",0.56],["2/23/Walls_of_ancient_port_-_panoramio.jpg","Walls of ancient port","Stanisław Ludwiński","CC BY-SA 3.0",0.57],["a/ac/Orikum%2C_Vlor%C3%AB%2C_Albania_2019_03_%E2%80%93_Marmiroi_Church.jpg","Orikum, Vlorë, Albania 2019 03 – Marmiroi Church","Pasztilla aka Attila Terbócs","CC BY-SA 4.0",0.66],["9/9d/Orikum%2C_Vlor%C3%AB%2C_Albania_2019_12_%E2%80%93_Marmiroi.jpg","Orikum, Vlorë, Albania 2019 12 – Marmiroi","Pasztilla aka Attila Terbócs","CC BY-SA 4.0",0.66]],"a":["Orikum","Orikum is a town and a former municipality in Vlorë County, southwestern Albania. With the 2015 local government reform, it became a subdivision of the municipality Vlorë. It was named after the ancient city Oricum, which was located 4 km west of modern Orikum. The population at the 2011 census was 5,503. The municipal unit consists of the town Orikum and the villages Dukat Fushë, Dukat, Tragjas and Radhimë."]},"2843":{"n":"Marina di Otranto","p":[["3/31/Otranto-San_Pierro01.jpg","Otranto-San Pierro01","User:Nikater","CC BY-SA 3.0",1.33],["f/f7/Otranto-San_Pietro02.jpg","Otranto-San Pietro02","User:Nikater","CC BY-SA 3.0",0.77],["0/06/Otranto_%2C_Puglia_-_panoramio.jpg","Otranto , Puglia","Patrick Nouhailler's…","CC BY-SA 3.0",0.67],["9/9b/Otrante_-_panoramio_%281%29.jpg","Otrante","Patrick Nouhailler's…","CC BY-SA 3.0",1.5],["7/76/Otranto%2C_chiesa_bizantina_di_San_Pietro._-_panoramio.jpg","Otranto, chiesa bizantina di San Pietro.","Carlo Pelagalli","CC BY-SA 3.0",0.67],["5/54/Otranto%2C_le_mura._-_panoramio.jpg","Otranto, le mura.","Carlo Pelagalli","CC BY-SA 3.0",0.67]],"a":["Otranto Cathedral","Otranto Cathedral is a Roman Catholic cathedral in the Italian city of Otranto, dedicated to the Annunciation of the Virgin Mary. It is the archiepiscopal seat of the Archdiocese of Otranto. The cathedral was consecrated in 1088. It is 54 metres long by 25 metres wide and is built on 42 monolithic granite and marble columns from unknown quarries. Its plan is a three-aisled nave with an apsidal east end. On either side of the west façade are two lancet windows.\nThe most famous feature of the cathedral is the 12th-century floor mosaic covering the entire floor of the nave, the sanctuary and the apse, which is one of the best to survive."]}}}